	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Raw strings (<code>"""</code>) without templates. squill puts the
			quotes on their own lines and lays the query out one clause per line
			between them. Plain strings are left exactly as written, and so is any
			string with a <code>$name</code> or <code>${"${…}"}</code> template in
			it &mdash; SQL with holes in it isn&rsquo;t SQL squill can parse.
		</p>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>kotlin</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query finds a
			raw string passed straight to a JDBC, Spring, or Exposed call:{" "}
			<code>query</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>exec</code>,{" "}
			<code>prepareStatement</code>, and the rest &mdash; with or without{" "}
			<code>.trimIndent()</code> on the end. Set{" "}
			<code>question-params = true</code> in the rule, as below, so
			JDBC&rsquo;s <code>?</code> placeholders parse as parameters rather
			than Postgres operators.
		</p>
	</section>

